INT. TOWN HALL - EVENING

Folding chairs. A banner reads "SAVE THE HARBOR FERRY." A coffee urn leaks in the corner. Rain on the windows.

MAYOR GWENDOLYN ARDITO, 50s, stands at a podium. Three citizens remain: BARNABAS KOWALCZYK, 60s, in a fisherman's sweater; TILLIE OSEI, 20s, with a clipboard; DOT PELLEGRINI, 70s, in a raincoat, knitting; and, in the back, a STRANGER in a damp suit, notebook closed on his knee.

GWENDOLYN
The council voted six to one. The ferry stops running on the first.

BARNABAS
Six to one.

TILLIE
(to the room)
We can still petition.

DOT
(without looking up)
We petitioned in 1998.

TILLIE
We could pray.

BARNABAS
Pray on your own time.

GWENDOLYN
(wearily)
I fought for this ferry, Barnabas.

BARNABAS
Then fight harder.

She says nothing. Dot's needles click.

The Stranger raises a hand.

STRANGER
If I might.

GWENDOLYN
And you are?

STRANGER
Irving Tse. Regional transit.

A murmur. Tillie sits up. Barnabas folds his arms.

IRVING
I'm not here to close it.

TILLIE
Then why?

IRVING
(mutters, to himself)
Because somebody has to say the numbers out loud.

TILLIE
Say them.

IRVING
It costs four hundred thousand a year. It carries eleven people a day.

DOT
(still knitting)
Twelve, when I'm visiting my sister.

Laughter, thin and tired. Gwendolyn rubs her eyes.

IRVING
(to the room)
I can keep it running one more year, if the town raises forty thousand.

BARNABAS
We don't have forty thousand.

TILLIE
We can have it.

BARNABAS
(grudging)
Amen.

Dot ties off her stitch, rises, and drops a knitted scarf in the middle of the table.

DOT
Starting bid. Sell it.

One by one, they reach for their wallets.
